## 4. Our understanding & our solution
<!-- Two failures this section exists to prevent: restating the buyer's own words back as
     "understanding", and proposing a method that never names the challenge it resolves. One row
     per client challenge — theirs, ours, and the honest verdict on whether we close it. -->

**Our reading of the problem behind the tender** *(3–5 sentences, in OUR words — the diagnosis,
not a paraphrase; if we can't say something the RFP doesn't already say, we haven't understood it)*

| # | Their challenge (cited) | Our understanding of what's actually driving it | Our solution / approach | Does it resolve it? | Best-practice / standard | Method provenance | Proof we can do it |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| <n> | `[RFP §x]` | | | fully / partly / no — <why> | <named framework + version / clause> | BR-nnn / A-nnn / exact primary URL | A-nnn / → §7 |

**Solution shape** *(how the pieces fit — workstreams, sequence, and the one design decision that
distinguishes our answer from the obvious one)*

**What our solution does NOT solve** *(state it. An evaluator finds the gap anyway; naming it first
is credibility, and it is the honest input to risk ownership)*
- <challenge left open, and what we propose instead — a phase 2, a client-side action, an assumption>

**Alignment check** — each solution element against (a) the requirement it answers, (b) the
methodology/asset we run it with, (c) the standard it conforms to. A row missing (b) is a
capability we're asserting without a method; missing (c) is an opinion dressed as best practice.
Naming a standard is not provenance: the adjacent source must resolve to an indexed asset, a
closed research row, or an exact primary-source URL.

